# [IOTOPS](../wg/iotops.html)

## Summary

The IOTOPS Working Group met to discuss active working group drafts, proposed adoption candidates, and potential new work items. Key updates were presented on IoT DNS security and privacy guidelines, updates to Manufacturer Usage Description (MUD) URLs, YANG ownership and licensing statements, Privacy Preference Declarations (PPD) for home networks, and MUD-based Remote Attestation Procedures (RATS) resource discovery. The working group also agreed on a path forward for restructuring the IoT security enabling technologies summary document.

## Key Discussion Points

### Administrivia & Working Group Status
- The session was chaired by Henk Birkholz and Alexey Melnikov. Notes were taken by Christian Amsüss, with action items captured by Alexey Melnikov.
- Slides for the introductory remarks are available in the [Chair Slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iotops-chair-slides-01).

### IoT DNS Security and Privacy Guidelines
- Tim Chown presented an update on [draft-ietf-iotops-iot-dns-guidelines](https://datatracker.ietf.org/draft-ietf-iotops-iot-dns-guidelines/) (version -07), detailed in the [IoT DNS Security and Privacy Guidelines -07 Review](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iotops-iot-dns-security-and-privacy-guidelines-07-review-00) slides.
- **Updates in -07**:
  - Addressed review feedback from Eliot Lear, Martin, Steven Farrell, Yaron Sheffer, and Behcet Sarikaya.
  - Clarified section relevance for stub resolvers (devices), local network resolvers, and authoritative servers (manufacturers). Added a threat summary table and terminology section.
  - Provided additional rationale against hardcoded resolvers, specified guidance on DNS server selection repetition, Discovery of Designated Resolvers (DDR), entropy/randomization, answer caching, and configurable TTL limits.
  - Added a dedicated section on DNS over CoAP (DoC) based on feedback from Martin.
  - Refined DNSSEC guidance on handling validation failures without degrading security, CA/software update mechanisms, and normative language ("should" vs. "must" with explicit exceptions).
  - Clarified that multicast DNS (mDNS) is out of scope and defined "network operator" as the entity running the local network.
- **Discussion**:
  - Alexey Melnikov and Henk Birkholz supported issuing a short Working Group Last Call (WGLC) (1–2 weeks) to verify that reviewer comments were adequately addressed.
  - Michael Richardson noted in the chat that while a second WGLC may not be strictly necessary, it is acceptable.

### Authorized Update to MUD URLs
- Michael Richardson provided a brief verbal status update on [draft-ietf-iotops-mud-acceptable-urls](https://datatracker.ietf.org/draft-ietf-iotops-mud-acceptable-urls/).
- The document is currently in WGLC (ending early December).
- The draft specifies a normative update to RFC 8520 regarding how MUD URLs are interpreted and updated by devices, including a two-step process for handling organizational acquisitions.
- The document has been stable and is expected to progress to the IESG following the conclusion of WGLC.

### Ownership and Licensing Statements in YANG
- Carsten Bormann and Alexey Melnikov discussed the status of [draft-ietf-iotops-ol](https://datatracker.ietf.org/draft-ietf-iotops-ol/).
- WGLC has completed, and review comments have been addressed in pull requests.
- Once the pull request is merged and a new revision is published, the chairs will progress the document to the IESG.

### Privacy Preference Declarations (PPD) for Home Networks
- Daniel Smullen presented an update on the Privacy Preference Declarations draft series, as shown in the [Privacy Preference Declarations for Home Networks (IETF 127 Update)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iotops-privacy-preference-declarations-for-home-networks-ietf-127-update-00) slides.
- **Technical & Architectural Updates**:
  - Architecture draft updated to support a single baseline configured endpoint (local discovery via mDNS or DHCP hints is optional).
  - Protocol updated to use standard building blocks: RFC 8615 well-known URIs and RFC 9457 problem details for errors.
  - The taxonomy draft remained steady, confirming stability of the core vocabulary.
- **Deployment & Incentives Discussion**:
  - Addressed feedback regarding vendor adoption incentives. Research indicates consumers are willing to pay a premium for enhanced privacy controls.
  - Clarified the gateway-first deployment model: home gateways implement policy enforcement first, allowing non-PPD devices to operate per local policy while PPD-compliant devices join incrementally.
  - PPD is designed to operate alongside legal frameworks (e.g., GDPR) without depending on specific regulatory mandates.
- **Q&A**:
  - Tim Chown and James Mozley supported adopting the work, noting potential interest from other SDOs such as ETSI.
  - Christian Amsüss suggested engaging open-source software projects (e.g., Debian popcon) as early adopters.
  - Jennifer Moufarrej inquired about alignment with initiatives like the US Cyber Trust Mark; Daniel Smullen confirmed PPD can serve as an enabling tool for compliance frameworks.
  - Esko Dijk asked about support for CoAP, Matter, and intermediary devices (such as 6LoWPAN border routers); Daniel Smullen confirmed these can be addressed in future protocol profiles or intermediary mechanisms.
- **Session Polls**:
  - *Poll 1*: Who has read the PPD architecture draft?
    - Results: yes: 5, no: 4, no_opinion: 5 (total: 28)
  - *Poll 2*: If you haven't read PPD architecture, will you read it?
    - Results: yes: 7, no: 1, no_opinion: 1 (total: 27)
  - Based on interest, the adoption call feedback window was extended slightly to allow for additional reviews.

### MUD-Based RATS Resources Discovery
- Henk Birkholz presented on [draft-ietf-iotops-mud-rats](https://datatracker.ietf.org/draft-ietf-iotops-mud-rats/), using the [MUD-Based RATS Resources Discovery (and some SUIT)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-iotops-mud-based-rats-resources-discovery-and-some-suit-00) slides.
- **Updates**:
  - Work resumed after waiting for dependent specifications (such as CMW collections / RFC 9396, EAT, and SUIT MUD) to mature.
  - Refined alignment with RATS terminology and mechanisms, incorporating CMW collections, key thumbprints, Epoch Bay/CoServ freshness mechanisms, and YANG augments.
  - Explored integration with potential "MUD extras" to simplify JSON expressions and module references.
- **Discussion**:
  - Daniel Smullen asked about appraisal policies. Henk Birkholz explained that while appraisal policies are out of scope for RATS core specifications, referencing signed policy packages (e.g., OPA Rego fragments signed via JWS/COSE) in MUD files fits naturally within IOTOPS scope.

### Security Enabling Technologies Summary / Document Structure
- Tim Chown, Alexey Melnikov, Daniel Smullen, Carsten Bormann, and Henk Birkholz discussed the future of the expired IoT security enabling technologies summary document.
- **Options Considered**:
  1. Maintain as a single comprehensive document with multiple section contributors.
  2. Split the document into smaller, modular drafts focusing on specific technical areas and device threat models rather than shifting regulatory requirements.
  3. Abandon the document.
- **Outcome**: The group agreed that focusing on technical requirements/mitigations rather than regulatory mapping is preferable. Daniel Smullen supported breaking the document into focused parts. Carsten Bormann highlighted potential relationships to T2TRG security setup work (`draft-irtf-t2trg-security-setup-iot-devices`).

